<h1>TActiveRepeater</h1>
<com:SinceVersion Version="3.1.9" />
<com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.ActiveControls.TActiveRepeater" />

<p class="block-content">
<tt>TActiveRepeater</tt> represents a data bound and updatable repeater control which is the
active counterpart to the original <tt>TRepeater</tt> control.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
This component can be used in the same way as the regular repeater, the only
difference is that the active repeater uses callbacks instead of postbacks
for interaction.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
Please refer to the original documentation of <a href="?page=Controls.Repeater">TRepeater</a> for usage.
</p>

<h2>Repainting on a callback</h2>

<p class="block-content">
A data bound control cannot push one value, because its whole body comes from the data.
Instead, assigning <tt>DataSource</tt> during a callback registers the control to render
itself again, and the response replaces the region in the browser. A handler does not call
<tt>render()</tt> for this, unlike
<a href="?page=ActiveControls.ActivePanel">TActivePanel</a>.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
public function callbackRequested($sender, $param)
{
    $this-&gt;List-&gt;DataSource = $this-&gt;fetchRows();
    $this-&gt;List-&gt;dataBind();
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<p class="block-content">
The repaint is subject to <a href="?page=ActiveControls.Introduction#ClientSideUpdates">the five conditions for a client-side update</a>.
</p>

<h2>The surrounding container</h2>

<p class="block-content">
To be replaceable, the control needs an element of its own to replace. It renders a
container around itself whose id is the control's client id followed by
<tt>_Container</tt>. That container, not the control's own markup, is what the callback
response swaps out.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
<tt>SurroundingTag</tt> chooses the element used for that container, and defaults to
<tt>div</tt>. Change it where a <tt>&lt;div&gt;</tt> is not valid in the surrounding
markup. <tt>SurroundingTagID</tt> returns the container's id, which client script needs in
order to address the region.
</p>
<h2>Connected pagers</h2>

<p class="block-content">
When the data source is assigned during a callback, the control also re-renders every
<a href="?page=ActiveControls.ActivePager">TActivePager</a> on the page whose
<tt>ControlToPaginate</tt> names it. The page count changes with the data, so the pager
would otherwise show buttons for pages that no longer exist.
</p>

